<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Francesca da Rimini</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1255–c. 1285</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Francesca da Polenta</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Francesca da Rimini, born Francesca da Polenta, was the daughter of Guido da Polenta, lord of Ravenna, and was married for political reasons to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-malatesta">Giovanni Malatesta</a>, called Gianciotto, of Rimini. Around 1285 her husband killed her and his brother Paolo, with whom she had a love affair. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a> made the lovers famous in the fifth canto of the Inferno, and their story inspired many later works.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francesca-da-rimini.jpg" alt="Francesca da Rimini" width="320" height="168" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dante Gabriel Rossetti · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dante_Gabriel_Rossetti_-_Paolo_and_Francesca_da_Rimini_(1862).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
